WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
The Cactoblastis Moth cleared 24 million hectares of Australian pasture and now threatens native cacti in Florida.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:11.360
This is unintended consequences, episode 171.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.320
Prickly Pear arrives as fence and fodder.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:19.740
A moth is chosen and reared against it that Moth later moves through the Caribbean into Florida

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.940
and the release rules still miss the roots that carried it.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:29.700
In 1926, the Commonwealth prickly pairboard set 3,000 cactoblastis cactorum eggs down at

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.320
Chinchilla station in Queensland.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:37.240
The larvae were meant to eat the pads of Opuntia stricta and Opuntia enermis, cactus

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.180
that had already taken grazing land.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.560
Within a decade, those stands collapsed.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:48.040
The same insect later crossed the Caribbean and reached the Florida Keys by 1989, and there

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.220
it began feeding on native Opuntia that had never evolved defenses against it.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.640
The cactus had not arrived as a pest.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.920
Early settlers brought prickly pear from the Americas in the late 18th and 19th centuries

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.160
to serve as living fences and drought fodder on arid holdings in New South Wales and Queensland.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.600
By the 1880s, the plants had escaped cultivation and spread across millions of acres.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.360
New South Wales answered with the prickly pear destruction act of 1884.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.120
Queensland followed with a reward scheme in 1907.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.080
Those measures were not enough.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:26.520
In 1920, the Commonwealth government formed the prickly pairboard and sent botanists abroad

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.140
to find insects that already attacked the cactus in its native range.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.560
The board's scientists came to see the South American moth, cactoblastis cactorum, as

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.780
a precise, self-sustaining remedy.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.580
A living control, they hoped, would cut the need for expensive mechanical clearing or

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:43.600
chemical sprays.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:48.980
At the time that reading was rational, the moth fed only on certain opuntia species.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:53.620
The Australian infestation was an introduced plant without its natural enemies.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:58.160
Earlier small-scale insect introductions against weeds had shown promise.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:03.360
Board members calculated that one successful establishment could replace repeated manual

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.680
labor across properties measured in tens of thousands of acres.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:12.120
Cutting and burning had already failed to keep pace with regrowth from buried pads and fragments.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.720
A moth that kept eating season after season looked like the first tool that might match the

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:18.880
plant's own persistence.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.200
After surveys by the Prickley-Pair Traveling Commission, the board imported a consignment

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.260
of cactioblastis from Argentina in 1925.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.820
The Chinchilla released the next year was the first in the field.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.000
What followed was mass rearing.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.960
At peak, the insectaries produced up to 14 million eggs a day.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:41.700
A single day's output thousands of times the first handfuls set out at that station.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:47.320
Field staff carried eggs across infested districts in Queensland and northern New South Wales.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:52.060
dedicated insectaries and a network of volunteer landholders collected and

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:56.880
redistributed larvae. In peak seasons the campaign moved millions of eggs each

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:03.460
week. By 1933 roughly 80% of the Queensland infestation and 50 to 60% of the

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:08.020
New South Wales infestation had been cleared. Contemporary reports from the board

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:12.280
described the moth as achieving complete control within 10 years and millions

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:16.460
of hectares went back to productive pasture. Not everyone in the agricultural

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:17.800
departments was settled.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:22.460
Skeptics asked whether the moth would stay limited to the target species once it was

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:23.700
released at those volumes.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:29.260
The board's own host-range tests conducted in Argentina showed feeding restricted to a

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.480
narrow set of Opuncia.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.520
That result met the regulatory threshold then in place, and the releases went ahead.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:41.100
The collapse in Australia rested on two conditions the tests had not been built to weigh at continental

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:41.600
scale.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.760
Effective predators were absent.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.960
The host plants stood in dense, continuous stands.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:52.180
Under those conditions, moth populations exploded and strip pads faster than the cactus

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.120
could regenerate.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.720
When the Australian Opancia populations collapsed, the boards work ended.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:02.600
The insect had already been shipped to other countries for the same kind of job, including

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.300
several Caribbean islands in the 1950s.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:09.960
Genetic studies later showed that a single founding event from those Caribbean releases

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.820
ceded the North American invasion.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:17.180
After the Florida Keys, the moth spread along the Atlantic and Gulf Coast to South Carolina

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:18.120
and Louisiana.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.300
Commercial plant trade and hurricane-assisted dispersal carried it.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:27.640
In that new range, it attacks native Opuncia species that support local wildlife and sustain

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:32.500
small-scale agricultural economies in Mexico and the southwestern United States.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.820
The insect disperses readily and lacks natural checks in North America, so containment

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:43.420
has focused on regulatory barriers to the movement of cactus material rather than on eradication.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.700
The traits that strip the Australian monocultures now do different work.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.860
A high reproductive rate, wind-borne adults, and a tolerance for fragmented host patches

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:58.400
let small founder populations establish on scattered native stands hundreds of kilometers apart.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:04.360
Land managers in Florida have to weigh the cost of protecting rare opuncia against another risk.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:09.820
Any surviving cactus patch can become a stepping stone for further spread in storm seasons.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:16.900
Once the threat to native cacti was clear, Florida authorities set up monitoring programs and public reporting hotlines.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:23.940
Early attempts at classical biological control using parasitoids from South America have met limited success.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.780
The regulatory emphasis has shifted to stopping further long distance transport.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:33.800
The moth that restored Australian grazing land now requires ongoing vigilance in the

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.060
southeastern United States and the Caribbean.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.600
A control agent crossed ocean basins and changed roles, from solution to problem.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:46.740
No reversal of the original Australian release was possible, and none was necessary.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:51.540
The unintended pathway opened decades later through separate introductions and through

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:52.220
trade networks.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:57.620
Current protocols in the United States treat any movement of opuncia material as a potential

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:02.980
vector. Inspection and certification are required now that would have been unnecessary before the

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:08.040
moth arrived. When the Commonwealth prickly pairboard chose cactoblastis cactorum,

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.860
host range and dispersal biology were known only inside South American and Australian conditions.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:20.100
The later Caribbean releases repeated that same narrow test. The insect then entered trade corridors

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.000
and weather corridors the test had never mapped, and those corridors carried it to Florida.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.780
A host-range trial ask what a moth will eat in the place where the trial is run.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:35.220
It does not ask where a fertile adult can ride once eggs, pads, and nursery stalks start

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.780
moving, or once a storm lifts insects off one island and sets them on another.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:41.860
The Argentine result was real.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.280
It was also local.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:44.840
Volume did the rest.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:49.320
Millions of eggs a week turned a confined feeding habit into a population that could

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.080
found itself again from a fragment.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:58.140
Two requirements follow from that gap, and they are requirements on the people who authorize a release, not on the insect.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:05.640
Before any continental scale release of a mobile organism, the project has to model secondary dispersal roots in advance.

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:11.580
Commercial roots and natural ones, named, not assumed away because the first host list looks short.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:18.600
And source populations need live inventories, so a new outbreak can be traced quickly to the introduction that started it.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:23.860
The way genetic work later tied the North American moths to one Caribbean founding event.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:28.700
Environmental intelligence spends its time on the regulatory side of introductions like

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:32.860
this, where a clearance in one basin becomes a vector problem in another.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:38.240
How might current proposals for insect or plant releases change if every project had to

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:43.440
map its likely movement through both commercial and natural pathways before the first specimen

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:44.220
left quarantine?

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:47.120
That's unintended consequences for today.

